  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25g-perchlike-fishes-7-remaining-groups/centropomus_unionensis_en/",
      "html": "<p>Centropomus unionensis grows to around 40 cm. It is a snook from the Pacific slope of Colombia. Suggested common name: Union snook.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25g-perchlike-fishes-7-remaining-groups/centropomus_unionensis_en/",
      "html": "<p>The snooks (Centropomus) are distributed in the New World; currently 12 species are recognized. Centropomus unionensis was imported for the first time from Colombia (Pacific slope). The species name refers to the type locality, Bay of Union, El Salvador.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25g-perchlike-fishes-7-remaining-groups/centropomus_unionensis_en/",
      "html": "<p>Snooks are rather marine fish, but many species enter brackish water regularly and some even pure fresh water. Among the latter is Centropomus unionensis.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25g-perchlike-fishes-7-remaining-groups/centropomus_unionensis_en/",
      "html": "<p>It is not certain whether this species can be kept long-term in pure fresh water, so adding some salt to the water is recommended.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25g-perchlike-fishes-7-remaining-groups/centropomus_unionensis_en/",
      "html": "<p>Specimens 5–8 cm long are very peaceful towards conspecifics and can even be characterized as schooling fish. Friends of unusual predatory fish will like this one.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25g-perchlike-fishes-7-remaining-groups/centropomus_unionensis_en/",
      "html": "<p>Before the recent past, snooks and giant perches (Lates) were united in a single family, Centropomidae. The largest known snook species reaches 120 cm, the smallest only 20 cm total length. Centropomus: ancient Greek, means “thorn-cover”, probably referring to the spiny operculum.</p>"
    }
  ],
